#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ^^^ for syntax checking in the editor only

##
## AgentsTools.InternOpSessionContextScan.include -- the --intern-op-session-context-scan op for
## DistroAgentsTools.fn.sh's dispatcher. Sourced lazily from that exact op's
## own dispatcher arm (not a wildcard -- this file holds one op only) via the
## standard $MDLT_ORIGIN-based path. Not standalone: relies on being
## dot-sourced for $1/$@/$MDSC_CMD.
##
## No --help entry: this is internal plumbing, the shared generic primitive
## underlying every --*-input-scan wrapper (--magic-grooming-input-scan,
## --magic-sweep-input-scan, --member-work-session-input-scan,
## --routine-coworking-session-input-scan, --magic-heartbeat-input-scan) --
## same relationship as AgentsTools.InternOpBoardUpsertMoveEdit.include has to
## --magic-grooming-to-*. Real scan policy (which states, which headers, by
## default) lives in each calling wrapper, never here -- this file only knows
## the generic scan/filter/format mechanics.
##
## CANONICAL NOTE -- per-consumer wrappers are deliberate; do not collapse them.
## --magic-advance-input-scan and --magic-grooming-input-scan pass
## byte-identical arguments to this primitive (the same five active states
## backlog/pending/running/blocked/parked, plus --all-types) and therefore
## produce byte-identical output on any successful run. That is the designed
## state, ruled so by the human-owner, not an oversight and not duplication to
## be refactored away.
##
## --magic-heartbeat-input-scan no longer does. It narrowed to
## running/blocked with a single `status` header, because routine-heartbeat's
## own steps consume nothing wider -- see that wrapper's own header comment in
## AgentsTools.MagicHeartbeat.include for the trace. It diverged unilaterally,
## which is precisely the freedom this per-consumer split exists to give.
##
## The reason is that these ops exist PER CONSUMER, not per scan scope. Each
## one is its own routine's own named input: routine-advance's,
## routine-grooming's, routine-heartbeat's. The op name is the stable interface
## a routine calls and that its own skillset file declares; the argument list
## behind it is an implementation detail that each routine is free to change
## on its own, for its own reasons, without negotiating with the other two.
## Collapsing them into one shared op -- or "deduplicating" two of them into
## calls to the third -- would couple three independent routines to a single
## argument list and make any later divergence a breaking change for consumers
## that never asked for it. Identical arguments today are a coincidence of
## three routines currently needing the same view, not evidence of one op
## wearing three names.
##
## Same "dedicated wrappers are fixed, not flexible" convention every other
## wrapper over this primitive follows: a caller wanting a different scan shape
## calls --intern-op-session-context-scan directly rather than adding override flags to,
## or merging, the fixed wrappers. Each of the three wrappers' own case-arm
## comments points back at this note.
##
## Note for anyone reconciling this against the board: the addendum on
## board/running/interview-2026-07-21-distroagentstools-board-scan-command.md
## asserting per-routine scan SCOPING is not accurate to this code -- the
## scopes are identical. Flagged, not edited here: board items are
## magic-coordinator's own to write.
##
## Output shape: one `## <state>/<item-filename>` line per matching item,
## then that item's frontmatter (either every field, or just the named
## --header values, in the order given), then a blank line -- grep/awk-
## parseable by design (same shape --magic-grooming-input-scan already had
## before this file existed; this file is that same logic, generalized).
##
## Three structurally independent filters -- deliberately not conflated into
## one mechanism despite all three living on this one primitive:
##   - board-item TYPE via --type-prefix:/--all-types, mandatory-explicit
##     (refuses to run with neither given -- no sensible universal default
##     exists the way one does for state/header below). Board-item type is a
##     FILENAME-prefix convention team-wide (task-*/note-*/inquiry-*/etc.,
##     see magic-team.armed.md's board-items entity model) -- --type-prefix:
##     is literally just a glob against the bare filename ("${prefix}*"),
##     nothing more; it never opens/reads the file or touches any frontmatter
##     field. Cheap enough to check before even stat-ing the file's content.
##   - OWNER (the `owner:` frontmatter field -- who's assigned) via the
##     optional --filter-owner <value>, exact match. A plain inline sed extraction +
##     bash string comparison -- too trivial (one exact-match line check) to
##     need a script of its own.
##   - --item <name> (optional, repeatable): restricts matched files to just
##     these bare basenames. Not part of this primitive's original spec --
##     added to make --routine-coworking-session-input-scan's "scan these
##     specific named items, wherever they live" need implementable as a thin
##     wrapper over this same primitive rather than reimplementing scan/
##     format logic independently; also used by --magic-sweep-input-scan's
##     own phase-2 (display exactly the phase-1-discovered survivors) call.
##     Harmless/inert for every other caller, which simply never passes it.
## These were separate requirements when this primitive was specified, never
## one combined concept -- kept structurally separate here for that reason.
## Checked cheapest-first: --item (bare string compare) -> type (glob, no
## file open) -> owner (the only one that needs to read the file).
##
## State/header defaults, at THIS primitive's own level (a caller going
## through a wrapper never actually hits these -- every current wrapper
## always resolves and passes its own explicit list first):
##   - no --state given -> every real board state (backlog/pending/running/
##     blocked/parked/processed/archived/retained) -- a sensible,
##     always-correct universal default ("no restriction" = "everything"),
##     unlike type.
##   - no --header given -> every frontmatter field, verbatim (via the
##     external AgentsBoardItemFrontmatterPrint.awk, shared with
##     --member-work-session-input-scan's own inbox-listing half) -- same
##     "no restriction = everything" logic, also always safe (more headers
##     shown than needed is never wrong, just possibly more verbose).
## Each wrapper supplies its OWN default state-list/header-list suited to its
## own routine's first-stage needs by always passing an explicit list rather
## than ever relying on this primitive's own bottom-level default -- "never
## one shared default for all" is enforced by convention at the call sites,
## not by this primitive refusing to have any default of its own.
##
## SESSION-CONTEXT SURFACE -- PARTIALLY BUILT, READ THIS BEFORE USING IT
##
## Target: one operation returning everything a session needs to start, emitted
## in the `# Session Sweep Report` shape defined by
## magic-team/templates/session-context.document.format.md and
## magic-team.shared.md's "Session-context document" entry.
##
## BUILT: the option surface and its validation --
##   --comms-since-date-time <YYYY-MM-DD...> | --comms-since-utime <epoch-seconds>
##   --comms-do-all | --intern-op-do-slack | --comms-do-email | --comms-do-trello
##   --intern-op-do-slack-tags  (an additional IM source, needs --intern-op-do-slack or
##                           --comms-do-all; declared and counted, not performed
##                           yet -- see its own block below for why)
##   --comms-member-scope-only  (restricts a requested comms scan to the acting
##                           member's own credentials and own configured
##                           sources; see its own block below)
##   --do-inbox-inquiry-active | --do-inbox-inquiry-all
##   --do-inbox-reflections | --do-inbox-notes
##   --do-board-related-active | --do-board-related-all
## plus the three comms sections themselves -- `## Incoming IM Updates`,
## `## Incoming Email Updates`, `## Incoming Trello Updates` -- each carrying
## real items, its own `instrument:`, its own `sources-scanned: N of M`, the
## format document's caps (128/128/64), and whichever of the three `**NOTE:**`
## forms actually applies.
##
## THE IM CAP COUNTS CONVERSATIONS; EMAIL AND TRELLO STILL COUNT ITEMS. A
## thread, channel or DM is one unit of the IM cap however many messages it
## carries, and the IM section reads oldest-first: conversations ordered by
## their own newest message, messages oldest-first inside each. Selection and
## presentation are separate on purpose -- the cap keeps the most recently
## active conversations, then renders that window chronologically. There is
## still no message-level merge ACROSS conversations, and the section says so.
##
## This diverges from magic-team/templates/session-context.document.format.md,
## which ratifies "128 IM" as an item cap and "newest first" as the sort. That
## document is not this file's to edit; the divergence is deliberate, follows
## the human-owner's own stated intent, and is raised with magic-librarian
## rather than silently carried.
##
## NOT BUILT YET: the inbox sections (--do-inbox-*), board-relatedness scoping
## (--do-board-related-*), and the report's own `# Session Sweep Report` /
## `## Contents & Abstract` envelope. Those flags are accepted and their
## sections ARE emitted, carrying the third `**NOTE:**` form ("no scan was
## made -- ... not implemented ... yet"), never the first ("no new X"). An
## unfinished scan must never be indistinguishable from an empty one, which is
## the whole point of the document.
##
## Per-service reality, measured, and how each is resolved here:
##   - Slack: accepts a cut-off (--oldest), offers NO unread semantics.
##     -> the cut-off IS the instrument; with none given the section says so
##        outright rather than naming a fallback Slack does not have. The
##        cut-off is NOT passed to Slack as `oldest` any more, though: a
##        server-bounded page cannot contain a thread parent older than the
##        bound, which made whole live threads undiscoverable. It is applied
##        CLIENT-SIDE instead, to the display blocks only -- see this file's
##        own commsCutoffTrimAwkProgram.
##   - email: offers UNSEEN, accepts no cut-off (no IMAP SINCE wired).
##   - Trello: offers read_filter=unread, accepts no cut-off.
##     -> for both, unread IS the instrument, and a cut-off that was requested
##        is reported as `cut-off-applied: no` rather than silently ignored.
## So the spec's "fall back to unread where the service offers it" resolves
## differently per service, which is exactly why `instrument:` is per section
## and not one line at the top of the document.
##
## NO LAGGING POINTER IS STORED, deliberately. It is the sanctioned mechanism
## where a service can take neither a cut-off nor unread semantics -- and no
## service here is in that position: the two that refuse a cut-off both offer
## unread. A pointer would add durable state, and a wrong-boundary failure
## mode, to buy nothing the service does not already give.
##
## THE CUT-OFF IS CONVERTED ONCE, AT ITS POINT OF USE (the Slack block below,
## where it feeds the client-side display filter and the thread-discovery awk --
## no Slack read takes it as a parameter any more),
## via AgentsIsoToEpoch.awk -- pure integer arithmetic, no date(1). There is no
## portable ISO->epoch via date(1) at all: POSIX specifies date only as a
## reader of the current time, and GNU's -d / BSD's -j -f are both extensions
## to a spec with no such feature. See that file's own header.
##
## Strictly additive so far: a caller passing none of these options gets exactly
## the document it got before this surface existed, byte for byte -- verified by
## diffing every existing wrapper's output across the change.
##
## This op is internal: no --help entry, not user-visible, and no skillset file
## calls it directly. Every option a site needs is passed by that site's own
## stub.
##
##

case "$1" in

	## <team-member> [--state <state>]... [--header <name>]...
	##   (--type-prefix:<value> [--type-prefix:<value>]... | --all-types)
	##   [--filter-owner <value>] [--item <name>]...
	##
	## THE LEADING <team-member> IS THE ACTING IDENTITY OF THE COMMS READS, and it
	## is a positional rather than a flag because that is this package's own
	## established shape for an --intern-op-*: --intern-op-slack-call and
	## --intern-op-check-slack-scopes both already take the acting member first,
	## and a third spelling for one concept is how a caller learns the wrong one.
	##
	## It is required on every call, not only comms-bearing ones. A board-only
	## scan does not use it, but making it conditional would mean the one
	## argument that decides WHOSE conversations get read is the one argument a
	## caller can forget -- and forgetting it read as somebody else's inbox
	## rather than as an error. Cheap to always supply; every wrapper already
	## holds the name.
	--intern-op-session-context-scan)
		shift
		## --context <caller-op>: see AgentsTools.InternOpBoardUpsertMoveEdit.include's
		## own copy of this block for the full reasoning. Pre-scanned before any
		## validation so every message here can name the invoking operation;
		## the option loop below consumes it again as an ordinary option.
		local opContext="<no --context given>"
		local ctxWanted="" ctxArg
		for ctxArg in "$@" ; do
			if [ -n "$ctxWanted" ] ; then
				opContext="$ctxArg" ; ctxWanted="" ; continue
			fi
			if [ "$ctxArg" = "--context" ] ; then ctxWanted="1" ; fi
		done
		local actingMember="$1"
		shift || true
		if [ -z "$actingMember" ] ; then
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): syntax is <team-member> [options...] -- the acting member is required, since it is the identity every comms read in this scan acts as" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		fi
		AgentsToolsAssertBareName "$actingMember" "<team-member>" "$MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext)" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }

		local states=() headers=() typePrefixes=() items=()
		local allTypes="false" owner=""
		## Session-context surface (see this file's own header note). Every
		## scope is opt-in and defaults to "not requested", which is a distinct
		## reported state from "requested and empty" -- that distinction is the
		## whole point of the generated document.
		local commsCutoffKind="" commsCutoffValue=""
		local commsDoAll="false" commsDoSlack="false" commsDoEmail="false" commsDoTrello="false"
		## An additional IM SOURCE, not a service of its own: the acting member's
		## own tag, searched across the workspace via search.messages. Off unless
		## asked for, because it costs a search call the rest of the IM read does
		## not, and because a member that is never tagged gains nothing from it.
		local commsDoSlackTags="false"
		## MEMBER-SCOPE-ONLY. Opt-in, off by default, so no existing wrapper's
		## behaviour changes. When on, the acting member's OWN Slack credentials
		## and its OWN configured conversations are the only things Slack may be
		## read with here: the team's shared SLACK_BOT_TOKEN fallback and the
		## team-scoped watched pair are both unreachable, and a member holding
		## neither gets a not-scanned section rather than somebody else's
		## channels rendered under its name. Email and Trello are already
		## member-scoped throughout and need no gate of their own.
		local commsMemberScopeOnly="false"
		local doInboxInquiryActive="false" doInboxInquiryAll="false"
		local doInboxReflections="false" doInboxNotes="false"
		local doBoardRelatedActive="false" doBoardRelatedAll="false"
		while [ $# -gt 0 ] ; do
			case "$1" in
				--state)
					if [ -z "$2" ] ; then
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --state: value required" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					fi
					case "$2" in
						backlog|pending|running|blocked|parked|processed|archived|retained)
						;;
						*)
							echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --state: invalid state: $2" >&2
							set +e ; return 1
						;;
					esac
					states+=( "$2" )
					shift 2
				;;
				--header)
					if [ -z "$2" ] ; then
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --header: value required" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					fi
					headers+=( "$2" )
					shift 2
				;;
				--type-prefix:*)
					local prefixVal="${1#--type-prefix:}"
					if [ -z "$prefixVal" ] ; then
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --type-prefix: value required" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					fi
					typePrefixes+=( "$prefixVal" )
					shift
				;;
				--all-types)
					allTypes="true"
					shift
				;;
				## Named --filter-owner, never --owner: this MATCHES on an item's
				## existing `owner:` frontmatter and skips non-matching items --
				## it is read-only and writes nothing. `--owner` as a write
				## ("set this header") is a different meaning entirely, and one
				## token must not carry both across this family.
				--filter-owner)
					if [ -z "$2" ] ; then
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --filter-owner: value required" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					fi
					owner="$2"
					shift 2
				;;
				--item)
					if [ -z "$2" ] ; then
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --item: value required" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					fi
					items+=( "$2" )
					shift 2
				;;
				--comms-do-all)    commsDoAll="true" ; shift ;;
				--intern-op-do-slack)  commsDoSlack="true" ; shift ;;
				--comms-do-email)  commsDoEmail="true" ; shift ;;
				--comms-do-trello) commsDoTrello="true" ; shift ;;
				--intern-op-do-slack-tags) commsDoSlackTags="true" ; shift ;;
				--comms-member-scope-only) commsMemberScopeOnly="true" ; shift ;;
				--do-inbox-inquiry-active) doInboxInquiryActive="true" ; shift ;;
				--do-inbox-inquiry-all)    doInboxInquiryAll="true" ; shift ;;
				--do-inbox-reflections)    doInboxReflections="true" ; shift ;;
				--do-inbox-notes)          doInboxNotes="true" ; shift ;;
				--do-board-related-active) doBoardRelatedActive="true" ; shift ;;
				--do-board-related-all)    doBoardRelatedAll="true" ; shift ;;
				--comms-since-date-time|--comms-since-utime)
					if [ -z "${2:-}" ] ; then
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): $1: value required" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					fi
					if [ -n "$commsCutoffKind" ] ; then
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --comms-since-date-time and --comms-since-utime are mutually exclusive, and neither is repeatable -- one cut-off, one spelling" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					fi
					case "$1" in
						--comms-since-utime)
							## Epoch seconds, with an OPTIONAL fractional part.
							## Rejected here rather than passed through, so a
							## malformed cut-off can never silently become
							## "scan everything".
							##
							## The fraction is accepted deliberately, not
							## leniently. The value a caller actually holds is a
							## Slack ts -- epoch seconds carrying microseconds,
							## e.g. 1786140114.450349 -- which is a utime, just a
							## more precise one. Digits-only rejected it outright,
							## so the first real sweep failed on the very value it
							## was handed.
							##
							## Truncating to whole seconds was the alternative and
							## is worse: Slack's `oldest` speaks this float
							## natively, and dropping the fraction moves the
							## boundary backwards, which re-reads the boundary
							## message on every single sweep, forever. Precision
							## is preserved end to end instead.
							##
							## Enumerated, never a [0-9] bracket range: ranges are
							## collation-dependent, and this validator is the only
							## thing standing between a malformed cut-off and a
							## silently unbounded scan.
							case "$2" in
								''|*[!0123456789.]*|*.*.*|.*|*.)
									echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --comms-since-utime: must be epoch seconds, optionally with a fractional part (e.g. 1786140114 or 1786140114.450349): $2" >&2
									set +e ; return 1
								;;
							esac
						;;
						--comms-since-date-time)
							## Leading YYYY-MM-DD is all that is checked here:
							## enough to catch a transposed/garbage value, while
							## staying free of any date(1) parsing, whose flags
							## differ between Darwin/FreeBSD and Linux. This
							## op never converts the value -- see the header note.
							case "$2" in
								[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-1][0-9]-[0-3][0-9]*) ;;
								*)
									echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --comms-since-date-time: must start with YYYY-MM-DD: $2" >&2
									set +e ; return 1
								;;
							esac
						;;
					esac
					commsCutoffKind="$1"
					commsCutoffValue="$2"
					shift 2
				;;
				--context)
					if [ -z "${2:-}" ] ; then
						echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --context: value required" >&2
						set +e ; return 1
					fi
					shift 2
				;;
				*)
					echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): invalid option: $1" >&2
					set +e ; return 1
				;;
			esac
		done

		if [ "$allTypes" = "true" ] && [ ${#typePrefixes[@]} -gt 0 ] ; then
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --all-types and --type-prefix: are mutually exclusive" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		fi
		if [ "$allTypes" = "false" ] && [ ${#typePrefixes[@]} -eq 0 ] ; then
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): exactly one of --all-types or --type-prefix:<value>... is required -- no implicit type default" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		fi

		## A cut-off with nothing to apply it to is a caller mistake, not a
		## no-op: it reads like comms were requested when none were.
		if [ -n "$commsCutoffKind" ] && [ "$commsDoAll" != "true" ] && [ "$commsDoSlack" != "true" ] \
			&& [ "$commsDoEmail" != "true" ] && [ "$commsDoTrello" != "true" ] ; then
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): $commsCutoffKind given with no comms selector (--comms-do-all, --intern-op-do-slack, --comms-do-email, --comms-do-trello) -- a cut-off applies to a requested comms scan, and requesting none is never implied to mean all" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		fi
		## The tag search is an IM source, so it needs the IM section to exist to
		## report into. Refused rather than silently ignored: a caller that asked
		## for tags and got a document with no IM section at all would read the
		## absence as "nothing tagged you", which is the empty-versus-not-scanned
		## confusion this document exists to prevent.
		if [ "$commsDoSlackTags" = "true" ] && [ "$commsDoAll" != "true" ] && [ "$commsDoSlack" != "true" ] ; then
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --intern-op-do-slack-tags is an additional Slack/IM source and needs the IM scan itself -- pass it alongside --intern-op-do-slack or --comms-do-all" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		fi
		## Refused rather than ignored, same reasoning as the cut-off guard
		## above: a caller that asked for member-scope-only and got a document
		## with no comms section at all has no way to tell the restriction was
		## honoured, and the one thing this flag exists to guarantee is exactly
		## what such a document could not evidence.
		if [ "$commsMemberScopeOnly" = "true" ] && [ "$commsDoAll" != "true" ] && [ "$commsDoSlack" != "true" ] \
			&& [ "$commsDoEmail" != "true" ] && [ "$commsDoTrello" != "true" ] ; then
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --comms-member-scope-only restricts a requested comms scan and needs one -- pass it alongside --comms-do-all, --intern-op-do-slack, --comms-do-email or --comms-do-trello" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		fi
		## --comms-do-all is the whole set, not a shorthand to combine with
		## part of it -- same fixed-wrapper discipline as --all-types.
		if [ "$commsDoAll" = "true" ] && { [ "$commsDoSlack" = "true" ] || [ "$commsDoEmail" = "true" ] || [ "$commsDoTrello" = "true" ] ; } ; then
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --comms-do-all and the individual --intern-op-do-slack/--comms-do-email/--comms-do-trello selectors are mutually exclusive" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		fi

		if [ ${#states[@]} -eq 0 ] ; then
			states=( backlog pending running blocked parked processed archived retained )
		fi

		[ -n "${MDAT_DATA_ROOT:-}" ] || {
			echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): MDAT_DATA_ROOT is not set" >&2
			set +e ; return 1
		}
		local boardRoot="$MDAT_DATA_ROOT/board"
		local frontmatterPrintAwk="$MDLT_ORIGIN/myx/myx.distro-agents/sh-lib/AgentsBoardItemFrontmatterPrint.awk"
		local headersJoined="" sep=""
		if [ ${#headers[@]} -gt 0 ] ; then
			for h in "${headers[@]}" ; do
				headersJoined+="$sep$h"
				sep=$'\034'
			done
		fi

		## ------------------------------------------------------------------
		## Which board items match, resolved ONCE, here, before anything is
		## printed.
		##
		## Two consumers need this same set, and the document's own section
		## order puts them in the wrong order to share it any other way: the
		## comms section prints FIRST but needs the open Slack threads recorded
		## on board items, while the board section prints LAST and is where
		## those items are actually walked. Collecting up front is what lets the
		## comms reader see them without either buffering the uncapped board
		## section in memory or emitting the sections out of the order the
		## format document fixes.
		##
		## The alternative -- a second filter pass private to the comms reader
		## -- was rejected: two copies of the same three filters, which must
		## agree forever or the document silently reads threads belonging to
		## items it does not show. One implementation, two readers.
		##
		## Filter order is unchanged and still cheapest-first: --item (bare
		## string compare, no file I/O) -> type (glob, no file open) -> owner
		## (the only one that opens the file).
		## ------------------------------------------------------------------
		local matchedFiles=() matchedStates=()
		local state f base it tp matched ownerVal
		for state in "${states[@]}" ; do
			for f in "$boardRoot/$state"/*.md ; do
				[ -f "$f" ] || continue
				base="${f##*/}"

				if [ ${#items[@]} -gt 0 ] ; then
					matched="false"
					for it in "${items[@]}" ; do
						if [ "$it" = "$base" ] ; then matched="true" ; break ; fi
					done
					[ "$matched" = "true" ] || continue
				fi

				if [ "$allTypes" != "true" ] ; then
					matched="false"
					for tp in "${typePrefixes[@]}" ; do
						case "$base" in
							"$tp"*) matched="true" ; break ;;
						esac
					done
					[ "$matched" = "true" ] || continue
				fi

				if [ -n "$owner" ] ; then
					## The read-bound here is deliberate and KEPT -- this is the
					## only filter that opens the file, so it must not read more
					## than it has to. What it was NOT is a bound on how much
					## frontmatter to consider: `sed -n 's/^owner: //p;q'` fired
					## its `q` unconditionally after line 1, which on a board item
					## is always the `---` fence, so the substitution never reached
					## an `owner:` line at all. Measured: rc 0 and a 0-line
					## document while 21 board-running items carried
					## `owner: magic-coordinator`. Same intent, correctly placed:
					## exit at the FIRST matching line rather than after the first
					## line of the file.
					ownerVal="$( LC_ALL=C awk '/^owner: / { sub(/^owner: /, "") ; print ; exit }' "$f" )"
					[ "$ownerVal" = "$owner" ] || continue
				fi

				matchedFiles+=( "$f" )
				matchedStates+=( "$state" )
			done
		done

		## ------------------------------------------------------------------
		## Session-context surface. Emitted only when a caller engaged it at
		## all, so a pure board-scan caller's document stays byte-identical to
		## what it was before this surface existed. Printed BEFORE any board
		## item, matching the format document's own section order (comms ->
		## inbox -> board).
		## ------------------------------------------------------------------
		local sessionContextEngaged="false"
		if [ -n "$commsCutoffKind" ] || [ "$commsDoAll" = "true" ] || [ "$commsDoSlack" = "true" ] \
			|| [ "$commsDoEmail" = "true" ] || [ "$commsDoTrello" = "true" ] \
			|| [ "$doInboxInquiryActive" = "true" ] || [ "$doInboxInquiryAll" = "true" ] \
			|| [ "$doInboxReflections" = "true" ] || [ "$doInboxNotes" = "true" ] \
			|| [ "$doBoardRelatedActive" = "true" ] || [ "$doBoardRelatedAll" = "true" ] ; then
			sessionContextEngaged="true"
		fi

		## Exit-status inputs for the scan-completeness verdict at the end of
		## this arm. Declared HERE, before the comms block, so the verdict is
		## well-defined even for a call that requests no comms at all: zero
		## sources asked for is zero sources missed, which is a clean 0 and
		## not a "nothing scanned" 4. Every --*-input-scan wrapper that passes
		## no --comms-* flag therefore keeps its present exit status exactly.
		local sessionContextSourcesTotal=0 sessionContextSourcesScanned=0

		if [ "$sessionContextEngaged" = "true" ] ; then
			local commsItemsAwk="$MDLT_ORIGIN/myx/myx.distro-agents/sh-lib/AgentsSessionContextCommsItems.awk"
			local isoToEpochAwk="$MDLT_ORIGIN/myx/myx.distro-agents/sh-lib/AgentsIsoToEpoch.awk"

			## Caps, per the format document: 128 IM, 128 email, 64 Trello.
			##
			## THE IM CAP COUNTS CONVERSATIONS, NOT MESSAGES -- email and Trello
			## still count items, and are untouched here. A thread, a channel or
			## a DM is ONE unit of this cap however many messages it carries.
			## Counting messages is what emptied the document of threads: thread
			## blocks are appended after both watched channels, a first-come
			## message cap kept whatever came first, and a 626-message no-cut-off
			## run spent all 128 on two channels -- measured, zero thread items
			## survived. The unit was the defect, not the number.
			local sessionContextCapIm=128 sessionContextCapEmail=128 sessionContextCapTrello=64
			## How many messages of lead-in context a trimmed thread keeps before
			## its first unread one, so a reply is never read without the message
			## it answers. Named rather than inlined because it is a judgement
			## call, not a derived quantity: three is small enough that a busy
			## thread stays readable and large enough to carry a question and its
			## setup. Inert without a cut-off -- with none there is no "first
			## unread", and the whole thread is already the answer.
			local sessionContextThreadContextBefore=3

			## Written as `if` statements, never `[ a ] || [ b ] && c`: that
			## compound evaluates to non-zero when both tests fail, and under
			## this script's `set -e` a false selector would end the operation
			## right here -- printing a partial document that reads exactly
			## like a complete one.
			local wantSlack="false" wantEmail="false" wantTrello="false"
			if [ "$commsDoAll" = "true" ] || [ "$commsDoSlack" = "true" ] ; then wantSlack="true" ; fi
			if [ "$commsDoAll" = "true" ] || [ "$commsDoEmail" = "true" ] ; then wantEmail="true" ; fi
			if [ "$commsDoAll" = "true" ] || [ "$commsDoTrello" = "true" ] ; then wantTrello="true" ; fi

			## Our own side of each requested comms source, for that section's
			## own `identity:` line. An unresolvable identity stays a stated
			## value here and never becomes an error line or a failed scan.
			local imIdentity="<unresolved>" emailIdentity="<not configured>" trelloIdentity="<unresolved>"

			## The cut-off is converted exactly once, here, at the one place a
			## reader actually needs the number -- and only for Slack, the one
			## service that can take it. --comms-since-utime is already epoch;
			## --comms-since-date-time goes through AgentsIsoToEpoch.awk, pure
			## integer arithmetic, no date(1) (see that file's own header for
			## why date(1) is not merely awkward here but impossible to write
			## portably). A conversion failure is a hard stop: a cut-off that
			## silently became empty would widen the scan to everything.
			local slackOldest="" cutoffEpoch=""
			if [ "$commsCutoffKind" = "--comms-since-utime" ] ; then
				cutoffEpoch="$commsCutoffValue"
			elif [ "$commsCutoffKind" = "--comms-since-date-time" ] ; then
				cutoffEpoch="$( printf '%s\n' "$commsCutoffValue" | LC_ALL=C awk -f "$isoToEpochAwk" )" || {
					echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD --intern-op-session-context-scan (internal operation, invoked via $opContext): --comms-since-date-time: could not convert to epoch seconds: $commsCutoffValue" >&2
					set +e ; return 1
				}
			fi
			[ -z "$cutoffEpoch" ] || slackOldest="$cutoffEpoch"

			## ---- Slack ----------------------------------------------------
			## Slack offers NO unread semantics at all, so the cut-off is the
			## whole instrument here and its absence is a real widening, stated
			## as such rather than papered over with a fallback that does not
			## exist. It is applied client-side rather than as Slack's own
			## `oldest` -- the runtime instrument string below says so, and this
			## comment must keep agreeing with it.
			local imBlocks="" imInstrument="" imSourcesTotal=0 imSourcesScanned=0 imPartial=""
			## imFailed means "not one source could be read". imAnySourceFailed
			## means "at least one could not" -- the weaker condition, and the
			## one the document-level aggregate note has to respect: a single
			## unread source already makes "no new incoming communications" a
			## claim about a scope nobody looked at.
			local imFailed="false" imAnySourceFailed="false"
			if [ "$wantSlack" = "true" ] ; then
				if [ -n "$slackOldest" ] ; then
					imInstrument="cut-off $commsCutoffKind $commsCutoffValue (applied client-side; Slack oldest= is deliberately NOT used, so thread discovery can still see parents older than the cut-off)"
				else
					imInstrument="none -- Slack offers no unread semantics and no cut-off was given; whatever conversations.history returns"
				fi

				## MEMBER-SCOPE-ONLY GATE. Resolved here, ahead of every Slack
				## read AND ahead of the identity resolve below, because both of
				## those fall back to the team's shared credentials on their own.
				local slackSourceNames=( magic-team human-owner )
				local slackOwnCredentials="true"
				if [ "$commsMemberScopeOnly" = "true" ] ; then
					## Presence only -- reduced to a boolean on the next lines and
					## dropped. No credential is compared, stored or printed.
					local ownTokenPresent=""
					ownTokenPresent="$( DistroAgentsTools --member-config-option "$actingMember" --select SLACK_USER_TOKEN )" || ownTokenPresent=""
					slackOwnCredentials="false"
					[ -z "$ownTokenPresent" ] || slackOwnCredentials="true"
					ownTokenPresent=""

					## The team-scoped pair is REPLACED, never added to: under this
					## flag the sources are the member's own configured
					## conversations and nothing else. SLACK_CONVERSATIONS is a
					## member-scoped config key holding conversation ids or
					## <channel>:<ts> targets, whitespace- or comma-separated.
					slackSourceNames=()
					local configuredSources="" configuredSource
					configuredSources="$( DistroAgentsTools --member-config-option "$actingMember" --select SLACK_CONVERSATIONS )" || configuredSources=""
					for configuredSource in ${configuredSources//,/ } ; do
						slackSourceNames+=( "$configuredSource" )
					done

					## Either refusal counts as ONE unread source, never as zero
					## sources: a zero denominator renders "nothing to read" and
					## "read nothing" identically, and the verdict at the end of
					## this arm would come back 0 on a scan that never happened.
					if [ "$slackOwnCredentials" != "true" ] ; then
						slackSourceNames=()
						imSourcesTotal=$(( imSourcesTotal + 1 ))
						imAnySourceFailed="true"
						imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- Slack not scanned, $actingMember holds no Slack credentials of its own; this scan never reads Slack under the team's shared credentials"$'\n'
					elif [ ${#slackSourceNames[@]} -eq 0 ] ; then
						imSourcesTotal=$(( imSourcesTotal + 1 ))
						imAnySourceFailed="true"
						imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- Slack not scanned, no conversation is configured for $actingMember"$'\n'
					fi
				fi
				## THE TAG SOURCE IS COUNTED, AND REPORTED AS NOT SCANNED.
				##
				## It is one more IM source, so it goes into the denominator the
				## moment it is asked for: `sources-scanned: N of M` is the only
				## thing standing between "nothing tagged you" and "the tag search
				## never ran", and a source left out of M renders those two
				## identically. Counted first, before anything can fail, for the
				## same reason.
				##
				## WHY IT IS NOT PERFORMED YET, stated rather than worked around.
				## The identity half of this is now solved -- the search runs
				## through --member-comms-slack-search-messages, which takes the acting
				## member and searches under that member's own user token.
				##
				## What is still missing is EXPRESSIVENESS, not identity. That arm
				## is target-shaped by ratified design ("TARGET-SHAPED, NOT A QUERY
				## PASSTHROUGH", its own header): it takes a conversation and builds
				## `in:<#channel> after:<date>` itself. A tag search is the opposite
				## shape -- a term naming the member, across every conversation,
				## with no `in:` restriction at all -- and there is no argument that
				## expresses it. Widening that arm into a query passthrough would
				## overturn a ratified decision, so it is asked for, not assumed.
				if [ "$commsDoSlackTags" = "true" ] ; then
					imSourcesTotal=$(( imSourcesTotal + 1 ))
					imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- member-tag search not scanned, no search term for a member's own tag is expressible through --member-comms-slack-search-messages (it searches one named conversation, not a term across all of them); no conclusion about tags is supported by this document"$'\n'
					imAnySourceFailed="true"
				fi

				## Own Slack identity, resolved ONCE per pass and only through
				## --magic-comms-slack-resolve-ids -- never a fresh auth.test
				## with a token read here. That op owns token selection, and
				## credential handling stays behind it even for a cheap lookup.
				##
				## It returns non-zero whenever it cannot confirm a reachable
				## human-owner target, even though AUTH_USER_ID is already
				## printed and perfectly usable -- so the `|| true` is what
				## keeps that non-zero from ending this whole operation under
				## `set -e` before a single section prints.
				local ownSlackId="" ownResolveOutput=""
				## Resolved for the ACTING MEMBER, not for a hardcoded
				## magic-coordinator. This id is what the widening test below asks
				## "was I tagged / did I post here" about, so resolving somebody
				## else's id answers that question about somebody else -- the same
				## identity substitution the two --intern-op-slack-check reads below
				## carried, in its third form.
				## Not called at all once the gate above refused Slack: this
				## resolver falls back to the team's shared credentials, so it
				## would answer with the TEAM's own id and this section would
				## print that as the member's own identity.
				if [ "$slackOwnCredentials" = "true" ] ; then
					ownResolveOutput="$( DistroAgentsTools --magic-comms-slack-resolve-ids "$actingMember" )" || true
				fi
				ownSlackId="$( printf '%s\n' "$ownResolveOutput" | LC_ALL=C sed -n 's/^AUTH_USER_ID=//p' | head -1 )" || ownSlackId=""
				if [ -z "$ownSlackId" ] && [ "$slackOwnCredentials" = "true" ] ; then
					imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- identity-widening skipped, own Slack user id could not be resolved; watched-channel and open-thread reads unaffected"$'\n'
				fi
				## Reused for the section's `identity:` line -- no second lookup.
				[ -z "$ownSlackId" ] || imIdentity="$ownSlackId"

				## Every thread this pass will read as replies, from two
				## independent discovery paths that both land here:
				##   - widening: a thread in a watched channel this identity
				##     posted in, was replied to in, or was tagged in
				##   - board: a thread an open board-item already tracks via
				##     communication-channel-id
				local threadTargets=() dt discovered
				local threadTargetsAwk="$MDLT_ORIGIN/myx/myx.distro-agents/sh-lib/AgentsSlackHistoryThreadTargets.awk"

				## ONE cut-off trim, used by BOTH read paths, held in a variable
				## rather than written out twice. The channel path calls it with
				## ctx=0 (drop everything at or before the cut-off, exactly what
				## Slack oldest= used to do server-side) and the thread path with
				## ctx=$sessionContextThreadContextBefore (same, plus that many
				## messages of lead-in before the first unread one). Two copies of
				## a cut-off test are two things that must agree forever, and the
				## document silently misreports the moment they stop.
				##
				## WHY THE TRIM MOVED INTO THIS FILE AT ALL: the fetches below no
				## longer pass --oldest, so nothing bounds them server-side any
				## more, and without a client-side filter every pre-cut-off message
				## would flood straight back into the document -- undoing the
				## cut-off fix a previous session landed and proved. Discovery
				## needs the unbounded page; display must not have it. Same bytes,
				## two different requirements, so the split is explicit here.
				local commsCutoffTrimAwkProgram='
					## Float-free ordering key: zero-padded seconds then exactly six
					## fractional digits, compared as a STRING. Concatenation always
					## yields a string type in awk, so > here can never silently
					## become the numeric compare a 22-digit key would not survive.
					## Returns "" for anything unparseable -- read that as "cannot be
					## dated", never as 0. An undatable block is KEPT: silently
					## discarding what this code cannot date would lose real content
					## to a parse gap.
					##
					## The empty-value case is REAL, not theoretical.
					## AgentsSessionContextCommsItems.awk prints `ts:`
					## unconditionally, so a message whose ts never parsed emits a
					## `ts: ` line with an EMPTY value. The test this replaced matched
					## that line, believed it therefore had a timestamp, compared the
					## empty value as 0 and dropped the block as stale -- the exact
					## opposite of the promise above. An unparseable value and an
					## absent line are ONE case here, and this function is the single
					## place that decides it.
					##
					## Not + 0: awk converts through strtod, whose decimal point is
					## locale-dependent, so under a comma-decimal locale every
					## sub-second ordering would collapse. The LC_ALL=C on every call
					## site is what stands between that and this key.
					function tskey(v,   d, sec, frac) {
						gsub(/^[ \t]+|[ \t\r]+$/, "", v)
						d = index(v, ".")
						if (d == 0) { sec = v ; frac = "" ; }
						else { sec = substr(v, 1, d - 1) ; frac = substr(v, d + 1) ; }
						if (sec !~ /^[0-9]+$/ || length(sec) > 16) return ""
						frac = substr(frac "000000", 1, 6)
						if (frac !~ /^[0-9]+$/) return ""
						while (length(sec) < 16) sec = "0" sec
						return sec frac
					}
					function flush(   i, t) {
						if (nb == 0) return
						t = ""
						for (i = 1; i <= nb; i++) t = t blk[i] "\n"
						body[++nblk] = t
						bkey[nblk] = curKey
						nb = 0 ; curKey = "" ;
					}
					BEGIN {
						cutKey = tskey(cut)
						## An unparseable cut-off must never widen the scan to
						## everything: with an empty key every real key compares
						## greater and the entire read comes back as new.
						if (cutKey == "") {
							printf("⛔ ERROR: AgentsTools.InternOpSessionContextScan.include: cut-off is not a parseable timestamp: %s -- treat as NOT SCANNED, never as empty\n", cut) > "/dev/stderr"
							bad = 1
							exit 1
						}
					}
					## This rule must precede the buffering rule, so a heading closes
					## the PREVIOUS block and then lands in the new one. Never next
					## here -- that would skip the buffering rule and the heading
					## would vanish from its own block.
					/^## / { flush() ; }
					{ if ($0 != "") blk[++nb] = $0 ; }
					## Offset derived from the colon, never a second hardcoded count
					## that has to keep agreeing with the pattern.
					/^ts:/ { if (curKey == "") curKey = tskey(substr($0, index($0, ":") + 1)) ; }
					END {
						if (bad) exit 1
						flush()
						## Oldest-first over a plain index array -- POSIX awk has no
						## asort and macOS ships BWK awk. Collected back-to-front
						## because Slack hands a page back newest-first, which leaves
						## this insertion sort linear on the real data path.
						m = 0
						for (i = nblk; i >= 1; i--) ord[++m] = i
						for (x = 2; x <= m; x++) {
							v = ord[x] ; j = x - 1
							while (j >= 1 && bkey[ord[j]] > bkey[v]) { ord[j + 1] = ord[j] ; j-- ; }
							ord[j + 1] = v
						}
						## Dated and undatable separated, so the context window counts
						## real messages instead of being eaten by blocks that have no
						## position in the ordering at all.
						nd = 0 ; nu = 0
						for (x = 1; x <= m; x++) {
							if (bkey[ord[x]] == "") { und[++nu] = ord[x] ; }
							else { dat[++nd] = ord[x] ; }
						}
						first = 0
						for (x = 1; x <= nd; x++) {
							if (bkey[dat[x]] > cutKey) { first = x ; break ; }
						}
						## Nothing newer than the cut-off means nothing new here, and
						## it emits NOTHING -- undatable blocks included. Emitting them
						## anyway would put a conversation with no new content back
						## under "New Incoming Communications" as though it were new,
						## which is the stale-parent regression this trim exists to
						## prevent.
						if (first == 0) exit 0
						for (x = 1; x <= nu; x++) printf("%s\n", body[und[x]])
						start = first - ctx
						if (start < 1) start = 1
						for (x = start; x <= nd; x++) printf("%s\n", body[dat[x]])
					}
				'

				## PER-CHANNEL IDENTITY MAP, for the per-thread reply fetch below.
				## "<real-dm-channel-id> <identity>" pairs, one per line, sourced
				## from the SAME "## dm=<id> identity=<name> ..." marker lines bug
				## 2's fix already reads the channel out of -- the identity is
				## right there in that same marker text, just never carried
				## forward until now. Built by the caller directly from
				## $slackJson, independent of AgentsSlackHistoryThreadTargets.awk's
				## own output shape: that script's $dt contract
				## ("<channel>:<ts>", nothing more) stays untouched, so board-
				## discovered threads (which never carry an identity) and this
				## map's own lookup misses both fall through to the exact
				## existing single-identity behaviour unchanged.
				local dmIdentityMapFile
				dmIdentityMapFile="$( mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/mdat-sweep-dm-identity.XXXXXX" )" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
				trap 'rm -f "$dmIdentityMapFile"' EXIT

				local slackName slackResolved slackChannel slackRaw slackJson slackBlocks
				for slackName in ${slackSourceNames[@]+"${slackSourceNames[@]}"} ; do
					imSourcesTotal=$(( imSourcesTotal + 1 ))
					slackResolved="$( DistroAgentsToolsResolveTarget "$slackName" )" || {
						imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- $slackName not scanned, no channel id configured"$'\n'
						imAnySourceFailed="true"
						continue
					}
					slackChannel="$( printf '%s\n' "$slackResolved" | sed -n 's/^CHANNEL=//p' )"
					## Unit-2 forward compatibility, INERT today. SITE 7 -- the one
					## that must NOT be left to construct "$slackChannel:" once the
					## channel can be empty: a bare ":" resolves to nothing and would
					## degrade to a `**NOTE:** partial` marker, which reads as the
					## system correctly reporting a known limitation and will be
					## believed. A partial marker must be reachable only from a
					## resolution that produced something and lost part of it, never
					## from one that produced nothing.
					local slackUserId=""
					slackUserId="$( printf '%s\n' "$slackResolved" | sed -n 's/^USER_ID=//p' )" || slackUserId=""
					## Captured whole, THEN stripped of its `## target=` header
					## line. Piping the call straight into `sed` would hand back
					## sed's own exit status, so a failed --intern-op-slack-check
					## would arrive here as a successful empty read.
					## NO --oldest, and this is the root-cause fix for thread
					## discovery, measured rather than reasoned.
					## conversations.history includes a message by its OWN ts, so a
					## bounded page cannot contain a thread parent older than the
					## cut-off -- however recently that thread was replied to.
					## Measured on magic-team at cut-off 1786250000: three real
					## threads carrying 41, 18 and 6 replies after the cut-off, the
					## latest as recent as 1786301763, were ALL invisible in the
					## bounded page. That also made AgentsSlackHistoryThreadTargets.awk
					## a filter over an already-filtered page: its `fresh` test
					## (latest_reply > oldest) is a tautology whenever oldest is set,
					## and the vaneHit bypass written to surface exactly these
					## threads was dead code. That awk is not the bug and is not
					## edited -- unbinding this fetch is what makes its own test mean
					## something again.
					## --identity-bot here, and only outside --comms-member-scope-only:
					## magic-team/human-owner are the TEAM's own shared sources, not
					## this member's, so reading them must not depend on whether THIS
					## acting member happens to hold a live SLACK_USER_TOKEN of its
					## own -- --intern-op-slack-call's default identity selection
					## prefers a present member token over the team bot even when
					## that token is the one that is actually broken, which is
					## exactly how this sweep silently missed both channels while
					## --print-acting-identity still reported "user". Under
					## --comms-member-scope-only, slackSourceNames is instead the
					## member's own configured conversations, which do need that
					## member's own identity to be read at all, so the flag stays off.
					## Bare "human-owner" (team-scope only) so
					## --intern-op-slack-check's own human-owner DM fan-out guard
					## (keyed on that literal bare word) is reachable -- passing the
					## already-resolved "$slackChannel:" bypasses it, verified live.
					## Member-scope-only keeps "$slackChannel:" unchanged: $slackName
					## there is a raw configured conversation, not the alias.
					local slackCheckTarget="$slackChannel:"
					if [ "$commsMemberScopeOnly" != "true" ] && [ "$slackName" = "human-owner" ] ; then
						slackCheckTarget="$slackName"
					fi
					## rc=3 is --intern-op-slack-check's own documented PARTIAL
					## outcome for the human-owner fan-out (one of its two DMs read,
					## one failed) -- its captured stdout already carries the DM that
					## DID succeed, and must not be thrown away the same way a real
					## failure (any other non-zero rc) is. Checked via a captured rc
					## rather than folded into the `||` above, so the two outcomes
					## stay told apart exactly as the op itself tells them apart.
					local slackCheckRc=0
					slackRaw="$( if [ "$commsMemberScopeOnly" = "true" ] ; then
						DistroAgentsTools --intern-op-slack-check "$actingMember" "$slackCheckTarget" --raw
					else
						DistroAgentsTools --intern-op-slack-check "$actingMember" "$slackCheckTarget" --identity-bot --raw
					fi )" || slackCheckRc=$?
					if [ "$slackCheckRc" != "0" ] && [ "$slackCheckRc" != "3" ] ; then
						imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- $slackName not scanned, --intern-op-slack-check failed"$'\n'
						imAnySourceFailed="true"
						continue
					fi
					if [ "$slackCheckRc" = "3" ] ; then
						imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- $slackName partially scanned, one of the human-owner's two DMs could not be read (--intern-op-slack-check rc=3); the other DM's content below is real, not the missing half"$'\n'
						imAnySourceFailed="true"
					fi
					slackJson="$( printf '%s\n' "$slackRaw" | sed '1d' )"
					## Feeds dmIdentityMapFile from this source's own raw text --
					## harmless when no "## dm=" marker is present (every source but
					## the human-owner fan-out): the awk below then simply appends
					## nothing. Real per-DM identity, straight from the same marker
					## line bug 2's fix already reads the channel out of.
					printf '%s\n' "$slackJson" | LC_ALL=C awk '
						/^## dm=/ {
							rest = $0
							sub(/^## dm=/, "", rest)
							id = rest ; sub(/ .*/, "", id)
							idn = rest ; sub(/^[^ ]+ identity=/, "", idn) ; sub(/ .*/, "", idn)
							if (id != "" && idn != "") print id, idn
						}
					' | sort -u >> "$dmIdentityMapFile"
					slackBlocks="$( printf '%s\n' "$slackJson" | LC_ALL=C awk -v kind=slack -v source="$slackName" -v channel="$slackChannel" -f "$commsItemsAwk" )" || {
						imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- $slackName not scanned, response was an API failure, not an empty channel"$'\n'
						imAnySourceFailed="true"
						continue
					}
					## DISPLAY IS FILTERED HERE; DISCOVERY BELOW IS NOT. The fetch is
					## now unbounded so discovery can see old parents, which means the
					## cut-off has to be re-applied to the DISPLAY blocks or every
					## pre-cut-off channel message walks straight back into the
					## document. ctx=0: a channel gets no lead-in window -- that is a
					## thread affordance, and this reproduces exactly what Slack
					## oldest= did server-side before.
					##
					## Applied BEFORE imSourcesScanned is bumped, same discipline the
					## thread path uses, so a filter failure counts as unread rather
					## than as scanned-and-empty.
					if [ -n "$cutoffEpoch" ] && [ -n "$slackBlocks" ] ; then
						slackBlocks="$( printf '%s\n' "$slackBlocks" | LC_ALL=C awk -v cut="$cutoffEpoch" -v ctx=0 "$commsCutoffTrimAwkProgram" )" || {
							imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- $slackName not scanned, cut-off filter over the channel page failed"$'\n'
							imAnySourceFailed="true"
							continue
						}
					fi
					imSourcesScanned=$(( imSourcesScanned + 1 ))
					## `$( )` strips EVERY trailing newline, block terminator
					## included, so a plain append glues this source's last value
					## line onto the next source's `## ` heading -- measured: three
					## items counted as two, which then drives imCount, the cap
					## check and the 0-messages decision off a corrupted stream.
					## The terminator is restored here. The `[ -z ]` guard keeps a
					## source that returned nothing from injecting a stray blank
					## pair, and the `|| ` form is what makes it `set -e`-safe.
					[ -z "$slackBlocks" ] || imBlocks="$imBlocks$slackBlocks"$'\n\n'

					## Identity-widening, from the history page just fetched.
					## Bounded to that page by construction: a thread parent
					## outside it stays undiscoverable here, and true
					## workspace-wide mention coverage needs a search-capable
					## token this does not have. Best-effort widening, never a
					## claim of complete coverage.
					## Captured, THEN guarded, THEN sorted -- never
					## `awk ... | sort -u` in one substitution. There is no
					## `pipefail` here, so a pipeline reports its LAST command's
					## status: the awk could print its `ok:false` diagnostic and
					## `exit 1` while `sort` returned 0, and this `||` handler --
					## the one that writes the `**NOTE:** partial` line -- was
					## unreachable. Measured: rc 0, empty output, no note. Same
					## capture-then-guard shape the --intern-op-slack-check read above
					## already uses deliberately for exactly this reason.
					discovered="$( printf '%s\n' "$slackJson" | LC_ALL=C awk -v channel="$slackChannel" -v oldest="${slackOldest:-}" -v vaneId="$ownSlackId" -f "$threadTargetsAwk" )" || {
						imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- thread discovery failed for $slackName, watched-channel read above unaffected"$'\n'
						discovered=""
					}
					[ -z "$discovered" ] || discovered="$( printf '%s\n' "$discovered" | sort -u )"
					while IFS= read -r dt ; do
						[ -n "$dt" ] || continue
						threadTargets+=( "$dt" )
					done <<- EOF
					$discovered
					EOF
				done

				## Open threads recorded on the board items THIS call matched --
				## same set the board section below will print, resolved once up
				## front. Scoping them to matched items is deliberate: a document
				## must never read a thread belonging to an item it does not show.
				##
				## The quote strip is load-bearing. These values are written by
				## hand and are inconsistently quoted in the real tree -- some
				## `communication-channel-id: "slack:D0BHQ3VTLB1:1786110788.609469"`,
				## some bare. A literal quote carried into `<channel>:<ts>`
				## produces a target Slack cannot resolve, and the read fails
				## per-thread rather than loudly.
				##
				## The value is `<service>:<rest>`, and only the three-field
				## `slack:<channel>:<ts>` form yields a thread target: a bare
				## `slack:<channel>` names no thread, and an `email:` value is
				## not a Slack thread at all. The target handed to Slack is
				## exactly that value with its leading `slack:` removed.
				## strip() removes the KEY prefix only -- `^[^:]*:` stops at the
				## first colon, and the key itself contains none, so the
				## colon-bearing value survives intact.
				## Skipped under --comms-member-scope-only: a board-recorded thread
				## is one of OUR conversations, so reading it as this member would
				## put team-side traffic into a document that names the member as
				## its whole scope.
				if [ "$commsMemberScopeOnly" != "true" ] && [ ${#matchedFiles[@]} -gt 0 ] ; then
					local boardThreads
					boardThreads="$( LC_ALL=C awk '
						function strip(v) {
							sub(/^[^:]*:[ \t]*/, "", v)
							gsub(/^[ \t]+|[ \t]+$/, "", v)
							gsub(/^"|"$/, "", v)
							return v
						}
						function flush(   n, f) {
							n = split(val, f, ":")
							if (n == 3 && f[1] == "slack" && f[2] != "" && f[3] != "") { print f[2] ":" f[3]; }
							val = ""
						}
						FNR == 1 { flush(); }
						/^communication-channel-id:/ { val = strip($0); next; }
						END { flush(); }
					' "${matchedFiles[@]}" )" || boardThreads=""
					## Captured, THEN guarded, THEN sorted -- see the identical
					## note on the thread-discovery read above. With `awk | sort -u`
					## in one substitution and no `pipefail`, this `|| boardThreads=""`
					## saw sort's status, never awk's.
					[ -z "$boardThreads" ] || boardThreads="$( printf '%s\n' "$boardThreads" | sort -u )"
					while IFS= read -r dt ; do
						[ -n "$dt" ] || continue
						threadTargets+=( "$dt" )
					done <<- EOF
					$boardThreads
					EOF
				fi

				## One read per distinct thread, whichever path found it.
				##
				## READ ORDER CARRIES NO MEANING HERE, which is exactly why this
				## is plain `sort -u` again. It was `sort -ru` because the cap
				## kept the first N blocks in emission order, so the order threads
				## happened to be READ in silently decided which threads survived
				## into the document -- selection steered from a distance by
				## something that should not have influenced it. Selection is now
				## explicit and lives at emission time (the grouping filter below
				## ranks conversations by their own newest message), so nothing
				## downstream depends on this order at all. `-u` stays for the one
				## thing it actually provides: de-duplicating the two discovery
				## paths, which routinely find the same thread.
				##
				## NO --oldest ON A THREAD READ, deliberately, and this IS a
				## behaviour change. Slack's `oldest` is a server-side lower
				## bound, so the messages a reader needs as lead-in context -- the
				## ones BEFORE the first unread reply -- cannot be fetched through
				## it at all: asking the server to hide them and then trying to
				## show them is not a thing. The thread is fetched whole and
				## trimmed locally instead, which is the only place both halves of
				## "several messages before first unread, and to the current
				## ending" can be honoured at once. The watched-channel read above
				## dropped it too, for its own separate reason (discovery cannot
				## see a thread parent older than a bounded page), so NO Slack read
				## in this file passes `oldest` any more and every cut-off in the
				## document is applied client-side by commsCutoffTrimAwkProgram.
				## PARALLEL FETCH: ~100 tracked threads meant ~100 sequential
				## conversations.replies calls here -- the real cause of
				## --magic-sweep-input-scan's multi-minute runtime. Slack has no
				## thread batch-read, so each thread is fetched in its own
				## backgrounded job instead; all shared accumulators are touched
				## only in the merge loop below, after `wait`. Not routed through
				## myx.common's Parallel() (lib/parallel): its eval-based arg
				## join drops empty positional args, and cutoffEpoch is routinely
				## empty. Capped via `jobs -pr` polling, not `wait -n` (bash
				## 4.3+; this platform ships bash 3.2).
				local threadFetchWorkDir
				threadFetchWorkDir="$( mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/mdat-sweep-threads.XXXXXX" )" || { set +e ; return 1 ; }
				## Combined with dmIdentityMapFile's own cleanup -- a bare `trap
				## ... EXIT` here would silently REPLACE the earlier trap set
				## right after that file was created, losing its cleanup rather
				## than adding to it (traps don't stack).
				trap 'rm -rf "$threadFetchWorkDir" ; rm -f "$dmIdentityMapFile"' EXIT

				## Written to a file (unlike the channel-level trim call above,
				## untouched) so a background job passes a path, not the awk
				## program's own text, as an argument.
				local threadTrimAwkFile="$threadFetchWorkDir/trim.awk"
				printf '%s\n' "$commsCutoffTrimAwkProgram" > "$threadTrimAwkFile"

				## Default 6, overridable -- unbounded fan-out risks Slack 429s
				## this scan never had to handle serially.
				local threadFetchWorkers="${ENV_MAGIC_SWEEP_THREAD_WORKERS:-6}"
				case "$threadFetchWorkers" in
					*[!0123456789]*|''|0)
						threadFetchWorkers=6
					;;
				esac

				## Real function, not inline, only because a backgrounded job
				## needs a callable unit -- single-consumer otherwise.
				AgentsSessionContextThreadFetchOne(){
					local memberName="$1" dt="$2" cutoffEpoch="$3" ctxBefore="$4" commsItemsAwkFile="$5" trimAwkFile="$6" workDir="$7" memberScopeOnly="$8" identityMapFile="$9"
					local dtChannel="${dt%%:*}"
					local outBase
					outBase="$( printf '%s' "$dt" | LC_ALL=C cksum | LC_ALL=C awk '{ print $1 }' )"
					local blocksFile="$workDir/blocks.$outBase" statusFile="$workDir/status.$outBase"
					local fetchRaw fetchJson fetchBlocks
					## PER-THREAD IDENTITY, derived from dtChannel via the map built
					## from the same "## dm=<id> identity=<name>" marker line bug 2's
					## fix already reads the channel out of -- NOT the single
					## whole-scan memberScopeOnly flag every thread used to share
					## regardless of which real DM it came from. A DM channel belongs
					## to exactly one identity pair, so a fixed flag applied to every
					## thread in a two-DM source fails "channel_not_found" for
					## whichever DM the flag didn't happen to match.
					##
					## Lookup miss (no marker for this channel -- every source
					## except the human-owner fan-out, and every board-discovered
					## dt) falls through to the exact prior behaviour unchanged: the
					## single memberScopeOnly-based branch below.
					local dtIdentity=""
					if [ -n "$identityMapFile" ] && [ -f "$identityMapFile" ] ; then
						dtIdentity="$( LC_ALL=C awk -v ch="$dtChannel" '$1 == ch { print $2 ; exit }' "$identityMapFile" )"
					fi
					fetchRaw="$( if [ "$memberScopeOnly" = "true" ] ; then
						DistroAgentsTools --intern-op-slack-check "$memberName" "$dt" --raw
					elif [ "$dtIdentity" = "bot" ] ; then
						DistroAgentsTools --intern-op-slack-check "$memberName" "$dt" --identity-bot --raw
					elif [ "$dtIdentity" != "" ] ; then
						## A non-bot, non-empty mapped identity ("user") -- omitting
						## --identity-bot resolves to memberName's own token, same
						## default --member-comms-slack-send-message's own doc
						## already states ("the member's own identity when it has
						## one").
						DistroAgentsTools --intern-op-slack-check "$memberName" "$dt" --raw
					else
						DistroAgentsTools --intern-op-slack-check "$memberName" "$dt" --identity-bot --raw
					fi )" || {
						printf 'FAIL:--intern-op-slack-check failed\n' > "$statusFile"
						return 0
					}
					fetchJson="$( printf '%s\n' "$fetchRaw" | sed '1d' )"
					fetchBlocks="$( printf '%s\n' "$fetchJson" | LC_ALL=C awk -v kind=slack -v source="thread:$dt" -v channel="$dtChannel" -f "$commsItemsAwkFile" )" || {
						printf 'FAIL:response was an API failure, not an empty thread\n' > "$statusFile"
						return 0
					}
					if [ -n "$cutoffEpoch" ] && [ -n "$fetchBlocks" ] ; then
						fetchBlocks="$( printf '%s\n' "$fetchBlocks" | LC_ALL=C awk -v cut="$cutoffEpoch" -v ctx="$ctxBefore" -f "$trimAwkFile" )" || {
							printf 'FAIL:cut-off filter over the reply set failed\n' > "$statusFile"
							return 0
						}
					fi
					[ -z "$fetchBlocks" ] || printf '%s\n' "$fetchBlocks" > "$blocksFile"
					printf 'OK\n' > "$statusFile"
					return 0
				}

				local uniqueThreads dt
				uniqueThreads="$( printf '%s\n' "${threadTargets[@]+"${threadTargets[@]}"}" | LC_ALL=C sort -u )" || uniqueThreads=""
				while IFS= read -r dt ; do
					[ -n "$dt" ] || continue
					while [ "$( jobs -pr | wc -l | tr -d '[:space:]' )" -ge "$threadFetchWorkers" ] ; do
						sleep 0.2
					done
					AgentsSessionContextThreadFetchOne "$actingMember" "$dt" "$cutoffEpoch" "$sessionContextThreadContextBefore" "$commsItemsAwk" "$threadTrimAwkFile" "$threadFetchWorkDir" "$commsMemberScopeOnly" "$dmIdentityMapFile" &
				done <<- EOF
				$uniqueThreads
				EOF
				wait

				## Merge after `wait`, same sorted order as before -- no
				## concurrent writer here, so no lock is needed.
				while IFS= read -r dt ; do
					[ -n "$dt" ] || continue
					imSourcesTotal=$(( imSourcesTotal + 1 ))
					local outBase statusLine blocksFile statusFile
					outBase="$( printf '%s' "$dt" | LC_ALL=C cksum | LC_ALL=C awk '{ print $1 }' )"
					blocksFile="$threadFetchWorkDir/blocks.$outBase"
					statusFile="$threadFetchWorkDir/status.$outBase"
					if [ -f "$statusFile" ] ; then statusLine="$( cat "$statusFile" )" ; else statusLine="FAIL:no status file -- job never ran or was lost" ; fi
					case "$statusLine" in
						OK)
							imSourcesScanned=$(( imSourcesScanned + 1 ))
							[ -f "$blocksFile" ] && imBlocks="$imBlocks$( cat "$blocksFile" )"$'\n\n'
						;;
						*)
							imPartial="$imPartial**NOTE:** partial -- thread $dt not scanned, ${statusLine#FAIL:}"$'\n'
							imAnySourceFailed="true"
						;;
					esac
				done <<- EOF
				$uniqueThreads
				EOF

				rm -rf "$threadFetchWorkDir"
				rm -f "$dmIdentityMapFile"
				trap - EXIT
				unset -f AgentsSessionContextThreadFetchOne

				[ "$imSourcesScanned" -gt 0 ] || imFailed="true"
			fi

			## ---- email ----------------------------------------------------
			## The inverse asymmetry: IMAP here offers UNSEEN and takes no
			## cut-off (no SINCE is wired), so unread IS the instrument and a
			## requested cut-off cannot reach it. Items are the unseen UIDs
			## themselves -- --member-comms-email-check is a check, not a fetch; a
			## session pulls one message's real content with --member-comms-email-read
			## <team-member> <uid> once it has decided which one matters, under the
			## same acting member this scan runs as.
			local emailBlocks="" emailInstrument="" emailFailed="false"
			if [ "$wantEmail" = "true" ] ; then
				emailInstrument="unread-semantics (IMAP UID SEARCH UNSEEN)"
				## The mailbox this scan reads as, from the acting member's own
				## scope. A member with no EMAIL_USER keeps the stated default.
				local emailUser=""
				emailUser="$( DistroAgentsTools --agents-config-option "$actingMember" --select EMAIL_USER )" || emailUser=""
				[ -z "$emailUser" ] || emailIdentity="$emailUser"
				local emailCheck emailUids emailUid
				## The ACTING MEMBER, not a hardcoded magic-coordinator: this
				## scan already carries the identity every comms read in it acts
				## as (see this op's own leading <team-member>), and the mailbox
				## checked has to be that member's own, exactly as the Slack
				## reads above already act under it.
				emailCheck="$( DistroAgentsTools --member-comms-email-check "$actingMember" )" || emailFailed="true"
				if [ "$emailFailed" = "false" ] ; then
					## Slack and Trello get their empty-vs-failed guard inside
					## AgentsSessionContextCommsItems.awk's END; email has no JSON
					## and so no such guard, which left this the one path where a
					## failed read rendered as a successful empty one. An absent
					## SEARCH response line is now a failure, never zero UIDs.
					##
					## The `sub(/\r$/, "")` is FIRST, and load-bearing: IMAP is a
					## CRLF protocol and awk's default FS does not treat \r as
					## whitespace, so without it the final UID arrives as `12345\r`
					## and is carried into the item heading and onward into any
					## --member-comms-email-read the session makes from this document.
					emailUids="$( printf '%s\n' "$emailCheck" | LC_ALL=C awk '
						{ sub(/\r$/, ""); }
						/^\* SEARCH/ { seenSearch = 1; for (i = 3; i <= NF; i++) print $i; }
						END {
							if (!seenSearch) {
								printf("⛔ ERROR: AgentsTools.InternOpSessionContextScan.include: --member-comms-email-check returned no SEARCH response line -- treat as NOT SCANNED, never as empty\n") > "/dev/stderr"
								exit 1
							}
						}
					' )" || emailFailed="true"
				fi
				if [ "$emailFailed" = "false" ] ; then
					while IFS= read -r emailUid ; do
						[ -n "$emailUid" ] || continue
						emailBlocks="$emailBlocks## email-message $emailUid"$'\n'"mailbox: INBOX"$'\n'"status: unseen"$'\n'$'\n'
					done <<- EOF
					$emailUids
					EOF
				fi
			fi

			## ---- Trello ---------------------------------------------------
			## Same shape as email: read_filter=unread is the instrument, no
			## cut-off is accepted.
			local trelloBlocks="" trelloInstrument="" trelloFailed="false"
			if [ "$wantTrello" = "true" ] ; then
				trelloInstrument="unread-semantics (Trello read_filter=unread)"
				## Trello's own identity, through the op that owns the Trello
				## credentials -- never a token read here. A failed lookup leaves
				## the stated default and does not touch the scan's verdict.
				local trelloWhoami="" trelloUser="" trelloId=""
				trelloWhoami="$( DistroAgentsTools --member-comms-trello-whoami "$actingMember" )" || trelloWhoami=""
				trelloUser="$( printf '%s\n' "$trelloWhoami" | LC_ALL=C sed -n 's/^TRELLO_USERNAME=//p' | head -1 )" || trelloUser=""
				trelloId="$( printf '%s\n' "$trelloWhoami" | LC_ALL=C sed -n 's/^TRELLO_USER_ID=//p' | head -1 )" || trelloId=""
				if [ -n "$trelloUser" ] && [ -n "$trelloId" ] ; then
					trelloIdentity="@$trelloUser / $trelloId"
				fi
				local trelloJson
				## The ACTING MEMBER, same reason as the email check above.
				trelloJson="$( DistroAgentsTools --member-comms-trello-check "$actingMember" )" || trelloFailed="true"
				if [ "$trelloFailed" = "false" ] ; then
					trelloBlocks="$( printf '%s\n' "$trelloJson" | LC_ALL=C awk -v kind=trello -f "$commsItemsAwk" )" || {
						trelloFailed="true"
						trelloBlocks=""
					}
					## Same `$( )` trailing-newline strip as the Slack append
					## above: without this the last notification's final value
					## line runs straight into the next section's own heading.
					[ -z "$trelloBlocks" ] || trelloBlocks="$trelloBlocks"$'\n\n'
				fi
			fi

			## Counted from the emitted blocks themselves rather than tracked
			## alongside them, so the reported number can never drift from what
			## the document actually carries. awk, not `grep -c`: grep exits 1
			## on zero matches, which under `set -e` would take the whole
			## operation down at exactly the "nothing new" case this document
			## most needs to report correctly.
			local imCount emailCount trelloCount
			imCount="$( printf '%s' "$imBlocks" | LC_ALL=C awk 'BEGIN { c = 0; } /^## / { c++; } END { print c; }' )"
			emailCount="$( printf '%s' "$emailBlocks" | LC_ALL=C awk 'BEGIN { c = 0; } /^## / { c++; } END { print c; }' )"
			trelloCount="$( printf '%s' "$trelloBlocks" | LC_ALL=C awk 'BEGIN { c = 0; } /^## / { c++; } END { print c; }' )"

			## Aggregate note: allowed ONLY when every requested comms scope is
			## empty AND was successfully scanned. A scope that could not be
			## read is unknown, not empty, and blocks this note outright -- the
			## parent must never assert emptiness over a scope nobody read.
			local anyCommsRequested="false" allCommsEmptyAndScanned="true"
			if [ "$wantSlack" = "true" ] || [ "$wantEmail" = "true" ] || [ "$wantTrello" = "true" ] ; then
				anyCommsRequested="true"
			fi
			## imAnySourceFailed, NOT imFailed: one unread watched channel is
			## already enough to make a document-level "no new incoming
			## communications" an assertion about a scope nobody read.
			if [ "$wantSlack" = "true" ]  && { [ "$imCount" != "0" ]     || [ "$imAnySourceFailed" = "true" ] ; } ; then allCommsEmptyAndScanned="false" ; fi
			if [ "$wantEmail" = "true" ]  && { [ "$emailCount" != "0" ]  || [ "$emailFailed" = "true" ] ; }  ; then allCommsEmptyAndScanned="false" ; fi
			if [ "$wantTrello" = "true" ] && { [ "$trelloCount" != "0" ] || [ "$trelloFailed" = "true" ] ; } ; then allCommsEmptyAndScanned="false" ; fi

			## Verdict inputs, taken from EXACTLY the counters the body prints
			## below -- imSourcesScanned/imSourcesTotal for Slack, and the
			## same emailFailed/trelloFailed booleans that drive those two
			## services' own hardcoded `sources-scanned: 0 of 1`/`1 of 1`
			## lines. Never recounted from the rendered document: a verdict
			## parsed back out of the text would be a second copy of the
			## truth, free to drift from the numbers the caller reads, and
			## the body is exactly what must not change here.
			if [ "$wantSlack" = "true" ] ; then
				sessionContextSourcesTotal=$(( sessionContextSourcesTotal + imSourcesTotal ))
				sessionContextSourcesScanned=$(( sessionContextSourcesScanned + imSourcesScanned ))
			fi
			if [ "$wantEmail" = "true" ] ; then
				sessionContextSourcesTotal=$(( sessionContextSourcesTotal + 1 ))
				[ "$emailFailed" = "true" ] || sessionContextSourcesScanned=$(( sessionContextSourcesScanned + 1 ))
			fi
			if [ "$wantTrello" = "true" ] ; then
				sessionContextSourcesTotal=$(( sessionContextSourcesTotal + 1 ))
				[ "$trelloFailed" = "true" ] || sessionContextSourcesScanned=$(( sessionContextSourcesScanned + 1 ))
			fi

			printf '# New Incoming Communications\n\n'
			if [ "$anyCommsRequested" = "false" ] ; then
				printf '**NOTE:** not requested\n\n'
			elif [ "$allCommsEmptyAndScanned" = "true" ] ; then
				printf '**NOTE:** no new incoming communications\n\n'
			fi

			printf '## Incoming IM Updates\n\n'
			if [ "$wantSlack" != "true" ] ; then
				printf '**NOTE:** not requested\n\n'
			elif [ "$imFailed" = "true" ] ; then
				printf 'identity: slack %s (config: %s)\n' "$imIdentity" "$actingMember"
				printf 'instrument: %s\n' "$imInstrument"
				printf 'sources-scanned: %s of %s\n' "$imSourcesScanned" "$imSourcesTotal"
				printf '**NOTE:** no scan was made -- no watched Slack source could be read\n'
				[ -z "$imPartial" ] || printf '%s' "$imPartial"
				printf '\n'
			else
				printf 'identity: slack %s (config: %s)\n' "$imIdentity" "$actingMember"
				printf 'instrument: %s\n' "$imInstrument"
				printf 'sources-scanned: %s of %s\n' "$imSourcesScanned" "$imSourcesTotal"
				printf 'sort: oldest first -- conversations ordered by their own newest message, messages oldest first within each conversation (no message-level merge across conversations)\n'
				printf 'cap: %s conversations -- a thread, channel or DM is ONE unit however many messages it carries\n' "$sessionContextCapIm"
				## The cap no longer bounds document SIZE, only conversation
				## count, so the message total is stated outright rather than
				## being inferable from a cap that will now rarely fire.
				printf 'messages-read: %s\n' "$imCount"
				[ -z "$imPartial" ] || printf '%s' "$imPartial"
				if [ "$imCount" = "0" ] ; then
					printf '**NOTE:** no new incoming IM updates -- scanned %s of %s sources, 0 messages matched %s\n\n' \
						"$imSourcesScanned" "$imSourcesTotal" "$imInstrument"
				else
					## CAPTURED, THEN GUARDED -- never left as a bare pipeline.
					## The filter it replaced (`/^## / { c++; } c <= cap`) could
					## not fail; this one can, and under this script's `set -e` a
					## non-zero bare pipeline would end the operation right here --
					## after the section heading and every header line had already
					## printed, and before the verdict block at the end of this arm
					## was ever reached. The caller would get a truncated document
					## with a success-shaped absence of any verdict at all.
					##
					## Declared on its own line first: `local x="$( ... )"` takes
					## `local`'s own exit status, so the guard would never fire.
					## Same reason the imCount/emailCount/trelloCount declarations
					## above are split from their assignments.
					local imRendered=""
					imRendered="$( printf '%s' "$imBlocks" | LC_ALL=C awk -v cap="$sessionContextCapIm" '
							## Same float-free, locale-proof key as the per-thread trim
							## above, and deliberately the same function: two orderings
							## that must agree forever should not be two implementations.
							function tskey(v,   d, sec, frac) {
								gsub(/^[ \t]+|[ \t\r]+$/, "", v)
								d = index(v, ".")
								if (d == 0) { sec = v ; frac = "" ; }
								else { sec = substr(v, 1, d - 1) ; frac = substr(v, d + 1) ; }
								if (sec !~ /^[0-9]+$/ || length(sec) > 16) return ""
								frac = substr(frac "000000", 1, 6)
								if (frac !~ /^[0-9]+$/) return ""
								while (length(sec) < 16) sec = "0" sec
								return sec frac
							}
							function trim(v) {
								gsub(/^[ \t]+|[ \t\r]+$/, "", v)
								return v
							}
							function flush(   i, t, g) {
								if (nb == 0) return
								t = ""
								for (i = 1; i <= nb; i++) t = t blk[i] "\n"
								body[++nblk] = t
								bkey[nblk] = curKey
								g = (curSource == "" ? "(source unstated)" : curSource)
								bsrc[nblk] = g
								if (!(g in gseen)) { gseen[g] = 1 ; gname[++ng] = g ; gmax[g] = "" ; gn[g] = 0 ; }
								gn[g]++
								## An undatable conversation ranks HIGHEST, never lowest:
								## the cap must not quietly delete the one conversation
								## whose recency this code could not establish. "~" (0x7E)
								## sorts above any digit under LC_ALL=C -- which is another
								## reason that setting is not optional here.
								if (curKey == "") { gmax[g] = "~" ; }
								else if (gmax[g] != "~" && curKey > gmax[g]) { gmax[g] = curKey ; }
								nb = 0 ; curKey = "" ; curSource = "" ;
							}
							/^## / { flush() ; }
							{ if ($0 != "") blk[++nb] = $0 ; }
							/^ts:/     { if (curKey == "")    curKey    = tskey(substr($0, index($0, ":") + 1)) ; }
							/^source:/ { if (curSource == "") curSource = trim(substr($0, index($0, ":") + 1)) ; }
							END {
								flush()
								## ONE comparator drives both stages. A conversation is
								## ranked by its own NEWEST message: SELECTION keeps the
								## top `cap` of that ranking, PRESENTATION renders that
								## same window ascending. Two different keys would leave
								## the rendered order non-monotone in the selection key,
								## so a reader could not tell where the cap had fallen.
								for (i = 1; i <= ng; i++) gord[i] = i
								for (x = 2; x <= ng; x++) {
									v = gord[x] ; j = x - 1
									while (j >= 1 && gmax[gname[gord[j]]] > gmax[gname[v]]) { gord[j + 1] = gord[j] ; j-- ; }
									gord[j + 1] = v
								}
								## gord ascends by recency, so the cap keeps the TAIL and
								## never the head. Keeping the head is what the old
								## first-come cap amounted to, and it is what starved every
								## thread out of the document.
								keepFrom = ng - cap + 1
								if (keepFrom < 1) keepFrom = 1
								printf("conversations: %d shown of %d\n", ng - keepFrom + 1, ng)
								## Truncation is never silent, and never reads like an
								## unread source: a dropped conversation WAS read and does
								## count toward sources-scanned. Naming each one is the
								## point -- "something was dropped" is not a report.
								if (keepFrom > 1) {
									dropped = ""
									for (x = 1; x < keepFrom; x++) {
										g = gname[gord[x]]
										dropped = dropped (dropped == "" ? "" : ", ") g " (" gn[g] " messages)"
									}
									printf("**NOTE:** truncated -- %d conversations found, capped at %d; %d dropped as least-recently-active. Their sources WERE read and are counted in sources-scanned above -- this is a display cap, not an unread source. Dropped: %s\n", ng, cap, keepFrom - 1, dropped)
								}
								printf("\n")
								for (x = keepFrom; x <= ng; x++) {
									g = gname[gord[x]]
									m = 0
									for (i = nblk; i >= 1; i--) if (bsrc[i] == g) ord[++m] = i
									for (y = 2; y <= m; y++) {
										v = ord[y] ; j = y - 1
										while (j >= 1 && bkey[ord[j]] > bkey[v]) { ord[j + 1] = ord[j] ; j-- ; }
										ord[j + 1] = v
									}
									for (y = 1; y <= m; y++) printf("%s\n", body[ord[y]])
								}
							}
					' )" || {
						## `conversations:` is printed from INSIDE the awk, so a
						## failed filter would otherwise leave this section carrying
						## `cap:`/`messages-read:` and no `conversations:` line at
						## all -- a section shape no reader has seen, and one that
						## reads like the field was simply forgotten. The failure
						## path states the field as unknown rather than dropping it.
						imRendered=""
						printf 'conversations: unknown -- the grouping/cap filter failed\n'
						printf '**NOTE:** partial -- the conversation grouping/cap filter failed; IM item blocks are omitted from this document, though their sources WERE read and are counted in sources-scanned above\n\n'
					}
					## Guarded, so the failure path above does not also emit the
					## stray blank-line pair an empty render would produce here.
					[ -z "$imRendered" ] || printf '%s\n\n' "$imRendered"
				fi
			fi

			printf '## Incoming Email Updates\n\n'
			if [ "$wantEmail" != "true" ] ; then
				printf '**NOTE:** not requested\n\n'
			elif [ "$emailFailed" = "true" ] ; then
				printf 'identity: email %s (config: %s)\n' "$emailIdentity" "$actingMember"
				printf 'instrument: %s\n' "$emailInstrument"
				printf 'sources-scanned: 0 of 1\n'
				printf '**NOTE:** no scan was made -- --member-comms-email-check failed\n\n'
			else
				printf 'identity: email %s (config: %s)\n' "$emailIdentity" "$actingMember"
				printf 'instrument: %s\n' "$emailInstrument"
				printf 'sources-scanned: 1 of 1\n'
				printf 'cap: %s\n' "$sessionContextCapEmail"
				[ -z "$commsCutoffKind" ] || printf 'cut-off-applied: no -- this service accepts no cut-off; unread semantics used instead\n'
				if [ "$emailCount" = "0" ] ; then
					printf '**NOTE:** no new incoming email updates -- scanned 1 of 1 sources, 0 messages matched %s\n\n' "$emailInstrument"
				else
					[ "$emailCount" -le "$sessionContextCapEmail" ] || printf '**NOTE:** truncated -- %s items found, capped at %s\n' "$emailCount" "$sessionContextCapEmail"
					printf '\n'
					printf '%s' "$emailBlocks" | LC_ALL=C awk -v cap="$sessionContextCapEmail" '/^## / { c++; } c <= cap'
				fi
			fi

			printf '## Incoming Trello Updates\n\n'
			if [ "$wantTrello" != "true" ] ; then
				printf '**NOTE:** not requested\n\n'
			elif [ "$trelloFailed" = "true" ] ; then
				printf 'identity: trello %s (config: %s)\n' "$trelloIdentity" "$actingMember"
				printf 'instrument: %s\n' "$trelloInstrument"
				printf 'sources-scanned: 0 of 1\n'
				printf '**NOTE:** no scan was made -- --member-comms-trello-check failed or did not answer with JSON\n\n'
			else
				printf 'identity: trello %s (config: %s)\n' "$trelloIdentity" "$actingMember"
				printf 'instrument: %s\n' "$trelloInstrument"
				printf 'sources-scanned: 1 of 1\n'
				printf 'cap: %s\n' "$sessionContextCapTrello"
				[ -z "$commsCutoffKind" ] || printf 'cut-off-applied: no -- this service accepts no cut-off; unread semantics used instead\n'
				if [ "$trelloCount" = "0" ] ; then
					printf '**NOTE:** no new incoming Trello updates -- scanned 1 of 1 sources, 0 notifications matched %s\n\n' "$trelloInstrument"
				else
					[ "$trelloCount" -le "$sessionContextCapTrello" ] || printf '**NOTE:** truncated -- %s items found, capped at %s\n' "$trelloCount" "$sessionContextCapTrello"
					printf '\n'
					printf '%s' "$trelloBlocks" | LC_ALL=C awk -v cap="$sessionContextCapTrello" '/^## / { c++; } c <= cap'
				fi
			fi

			## Inbox scopes: still unbuilt. Reported with the format document's
			## own third NOTE form (asked, could not look) rather than the
			## first (looked, found nothing) -- an unbuilt scan must never be
			## indistinguishable from an empty one.
			local inboxSection
			for inboxSection in \
				"Active Inbox Inquiry Items:$doInboxInquiryActive$doInboxInquiryAll" \
				"Current Inbox Reflections:$doInboxReflections" \
				"Current Inbox Notes:$doInboxNotes" \
			; do
				printf '## %s\n\n' "${inboxSection%%:*}"
				case "${inboxSection#*:}" in
					*true*) printf '**NOTE:** no scan was made -- inbox scopes are not implemented in this operation yet\n\n' ;;
					*)      printf '**NOTE:** not requested\n\n' ;;
				esac
			done

			printf '## Board Items\n\n'
			if [ "$doBoardRelatedActive" = "true" ] || [ "$doBoardRelatedAll" = "true" ] ; then
				printf '**NOTE:** no scan was made -- board-relatedness scoping (--do-board-related-*) is not implemented in this operation yet; the item blocks below are this call'"'"'s own --state/--type-prefix:/--filter-owner scan, unfiltered by relatedness\n\n'
			fi
		fi

		## Filtering already happened once, up front -- this walks the resolved
		## set in the same order it was resolved, so the emitted document is
		## byte-identical to what the inline-filter version produced.
		local h val mi
		mi=0
		while [ "$mi" -lt "${#matchedFiles[@]}" ] ; do
			f="${matchedFiles[$mi]}"
			state="${matchedStates[$mi]}"
			mi=$(( mi + 1 ))
			base="${f##*/}"

			printf '## %s/%s\n' "$state" "$base"
			if [ ${#headers[@]} -eq 0 ] ; then
				LC_ALL=C awk -f "$frontmatterPrintAwk" "$f"
			else
				## A FENCE-LESS ITEM MUST NOT VANISH. This printer used to enter
				## frontmatter only on `NR == 1 && $0 == "---"`, so an item whose
				## `---` fence was missing yielded empty values for every
				## requested header -- and a downstream survivor filter
				## (AgentsMagicSweepSlackThreadNames.awk) drops an item whose
				## values are empty. The item disappeared from the document with
				## no diagnostic anywhere. Measured on
				## board/blocked/warning-20260807T0000Z-keeper-myx-deploy-status-
				## message-impersonation-suspected.md: phase A 126 blocks, 17
				## survivors, that item 0 -- while the board-thread extractor in
				## this same file DID produce its thread, because that one matches
				## `^communication-channel-id:` on any line and has no fence
				## concept at all. Two read paths, same file, opposite answers.
				##
				## Fixed in two parts, both needed:
				##  1. The opening fence is now the FIRST `---` anywhere, not one
				##     pinned to line 1 -- matching AgentsBoardItemFrontmatterPrint.awk,
				##     the sibling printer used by this op's own all-headers case
				##     three lines above. Those two disagreeing about where
				##     frontmatter starts was a defect in itself.
				##  2. A file with NO `---` at all falls back to harvesting the
				##     same `key: value` lines from the whole document. Part 1
				##     alone does not rescue a fence-less item -- with no `---`
				##     anywhere, "first fence anywhere" still never fires and the
				##     values stay empty. The fallback is what actually closes the
				##     silent drop, and it lands this printer on the behaviour the
				##     source-slack-* extractor already had, which is the reading
				##     that was demonstrably right about this file.
				##
				## The fallback is deliberately SECOND-CLASS: it is consulted only
				## when no fence was seen at all. A properly fenced item is parsed
				## exactly as before, so body prose that happens to look like
				## `key: value` can never shadow a real frontmatter field.
				LC_ALL=C awk -v headers="$headersJoined" '
					BEGIN {
						n = split(headers, order, "\034")
						for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
							vals[order[i]] = ""
							fallback[order[i]] = ""
						}
					}
					!seen_fm && $0 == "---" {
						seen_fm = 1
						in_fm = 1
						next
					}
					in_fm && $0 == "---" {
						in_fm = 0
						closed_fm = 1
						next
					}
					closed_fm { next }
					{
						pos = index($0, ": ")
						if (pos > 0) {
							key = substr($0, 1, pos - 1)
							if (key in vals) {
								if (in_fm) {
									if (vals[key] == "") vals[key] = substr($0, pos + 2)
								} else {
									if (fallback[key] == "") fallback[key] = substr($0, pos + 2)
								}
							}
						}
					}
					END {
						for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
							key = order[i]
							val = vals[key]
							if (!seen_fm && val == "") val = fallback[key]
							printf "%s: %s\n", key, val
						}
					}
				' "$f"
			fi
			printf '\n'
		done

		## SCAN-COMPLETENESS VERDICT. The body above already reports every
		## unread source honestly -- `NOT SCANNED`/`**NOTE:** partial` markers
		## and a `sources-scanned: N of M` line per service -- and it still
		## returned 0, so a human reading the output saw the gap while
		## anything branching on exit status saw success. The report was never
		## the problem; the verdict was.
		##
		## Codes follow the convention this package already ratified for the
		## human-owner two-conversation fan-out (--member-comms-slack-read
		## human-owner): 0 when everything asked for was read, 3 when only
		## part of it was, 4 when none of it was, 1 when the operation failed
		## before producing a document at all. Same three-way shape, same
		## meanings, so a caller that already handles one handles this.
		##
		## `set +e` before a deliberate non-zero return is this script's own
		## established idiom (see every `set +e ; return 1` above): the
		## callers of this op invoke it as a bare command and then
		## `return $?`, and under the function's own `set -e` a non-zero bare
		## command would end the shell before that line was ever reached.
		if [ "$sessionContextSourcesTotal" -eq 0 ] || [ "$sessionContextSourcesScanned" -eq "$sessionContextSourcesTotal" ] ; then
			return 0
		fi
		if [ "$sessionContextSourcesScanned" -eq 0 ] ; then
			set +e ; return 4
		fi
		set +e ; return 3
	;;

	*)
		echo "⛔ ERROR: $MDSC_CMD: invalid option: $1 (invoked via $opContext) -- rejected by AgentsTools.InternOpSessionContextScan.include's own default (*) branch, the operation group sourced by the --intern-op-session-context-scan dispatcher route; no operation of that name is defined in this include" >&2
		set +e ; return 1
	;;
esac
